00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.850
It's got to be everyone--

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:02.450
Uber, ordered something?

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.450
OK.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.910
So API are becoming
mainstream, right?

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:10.320
To me, there is no
more powerful fact

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.400
than saying it's entered
our each and everyday life.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.430
On a daily basis, you're
probably hitting more than five

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.920
or 10 APIs, at a minimum.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.080
In this world, when
people are talking

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.750
about data transformation,
enterprises

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.440
are morphing them,
themselves, into entities

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.500
where their products
are nothing but APIs.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.270
So if you transact your
capabilities as APIs

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:39.150
with your employees,
with their partners,

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:44.040
APIs become extremely
critical in how you are

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.380
perceived in the marketplace.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:52.080
For us, personally, we pride
in powering successful API

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.330
programs for many of
our key customers.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.700
And I'm really happy
to talk about that.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:02.600
We have nearly doubled
our API traffic

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:05.640
since March of last year.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.470
This is the amount
of API traffic

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:11.693
that we serve, just alone,
from our cloud platform.

00:03:11.693 --> 00:03:13.110
So let's talk a
little bit about--

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.720
because the APIs have
become so front and center

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.520
of an enterprises' face out
there in this world, what

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.580
that means is,
operationally, the teams

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.390
have to be on top of ensuring
that APIs are performing

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:29.480
the way they should.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.200
So no longer is it OK
for you to find out,

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.680
after the fact, that
hey, my API was down,

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.580
because it has downstream
effect of affecting many

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.220
of your partners who
may be banking on an API

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.750
to deliver connected
experiences of their own.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:51.520
So ops teams need to be able
to monitor APIs in real time.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.240
And, more importantly,
have the ability

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.370
to identify issues before
the consumers of the API

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.350
realize that there are
some issues out there.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.660
That's a very important aspect.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:07.860
And as you start to look
to some of these features,

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.340
you also want to be
able to quickly diagnose

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.070
where the issues are
so that you can lower

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.149
the mean time to resolution.

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:17.760
Now we do have many customers.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.490
And when I talk to them,
they use several tools

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:24.210
out there today to be
able to get a sense of,

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.290
is something broken?

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.890
And what we find out,
in talking to them,

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.430
that synthetic monitoring
tools just aren't enough.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.880
And what I mean by that is
these are typical API monitoring

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.180
tools which are sitting
outside the platform,

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.270
using some probes, to be able
to gather some key metrics--

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.470
such as suddenly there is a high
amount of error rate in an API,

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.520
or your latency just went up.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.970
So they can tell
you what's broken--

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:54.580
of course, that's
the whole purpose.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.650
But the key point
is, they are not

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.320
correlated with some
of the other things

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.800
that you need to be able to
perform useful forensics,

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.680
such as, can I go take
a look at the log file

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.970
right now at this moment to
understand, can I correlate

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:11.750
that, with a specific error?

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.980
So these tools tell
you what's broken,

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.080
but they don't really
give you the insight

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.540
into where it's broken
so you can quickly

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.280
fix what you need to.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.710
So Apigee's API monitoring is
an integrated API monitoring

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.640
solution-- what we also call
a white box API monitoring

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:31.990
solution.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.330
And so one of the
key advantages of it

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.330
being integrated
into the platform

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:43.630
is the ability for it to be able
to give you contextual insights

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.860
into your API issues.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.480
So let's have an example.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:48.940
If we have an error--

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.400
a higher error rate in
an API, suddenly-- you're

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:53.560
able to, through a
dashboard, visualize what

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:57.040
the error is, and then do
contextual drill downs to find

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.150
out the source of the error, and
also do some determinism on how

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:02.800
widespread the error is.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.590
And, actually, I will be showing
that to you in a quick demo.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:08.770
But the fact of
having API monitoring

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:10.750
integrated in the
platform not only

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.090
gives us the contextual
insight that we can

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.130
deliver to you, our customers.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.440
But more importantly,
it opens up

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.910
some very interesting
possibilities,

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:23.500
which are just not possible
through a synthetic monitoring

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:24.250
solution.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:28.330
Such as, imagine
a scenario where

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:33.340
you are the operational
team, and you're

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.920
using the Apigee Edge
platform, as an example,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.070
to serve the APIs
to your consumers.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:41.770
And let's say, for those
out here-- just a quick show

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:42.490
of hands.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:46.300
How many users in the audience
have used Apigee Edge before?

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.800
OK, quite a few.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:51.700
So Apigee Edge is a full
lifecycle API management

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.630
platform.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:55.150
And you can use out-of-the-box
policies to do things like

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:56.180
traffic management.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.540
Let's imagine a
scenario when you've

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.720
used one of the [? Spiker S ?]
policies to limit the amount

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:04.930
of traffic to one or more APIs.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:06.710
And you have set
it to a threshold,

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:09.530
let's say, 1,000 per minute.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:11.150
And maybe that sells--

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:12.920
you know, OK for your needs.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:14.900
And suddenly there's
a catastrophic failure

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:17.540
in your back-ends, the
back-ends which are

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.640
backed into the API proxies.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:22.160
And you want to be
able to, very quickly,

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.710
shut off traffic to them.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:28.322
Well, one choice is
you can go and update

00:07:28.322 --> 00:07:29.780
all the [? Spiker S ?]
policies one

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:32.960
by one, depending on the
number of API proxies.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.680
But by that time, your back-ends
may not be able to survive.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.918
So how about, if
we're able to lever

00:07:39.918 --> 00:07:41.960
the power of the platform--
and this is something

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.440
we are working on.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:44.870
And we give you
operator controls

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:47.840
to be able to dial down
the traffic to all the APIs

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.770
at once.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.840
That is what we mean by
the [? close-to ?] aspect

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:54.150
of the integrated
API monitoring.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:56.047
That is very powerful.

00:07:56.047 --> 00:07:58.130
And that is something which
a synthetic monitoring

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.190
tool can never provide to you.

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:04.205
So Apigee API monitoring,
as I mentioned,

00:08:04.205 --> 00:08:07.300
is integrated into the
platform, leverages

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.770
the best of Google tech.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:12.320
We have some icons out here.

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:14.990
Just a quick sampling
of the underlying tech

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.960
that we use to power
these capabilities

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.755
and deliver it to our customers.

00:08:20.755 --> 00:08:22.380
Precision diagnostics--
I'll just spent

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.090
a very few seconds on this.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:27.630
This is a very important piece
of lowering your mean time

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:28.920
for diagnostics.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.620
And this ties into
what I just mentioned.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:33.870
Of course, tools can
tell you what's broken,

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.480
but we tell you
where it's broken.

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:37.110
I'll show that to
you in a few minutes.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.809
Contextual insights--
this is essentially

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:44.159
where, once an issue or
an error is detected,

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:46.380
how do you really
take that, drill down

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:51.270
through different aspects, and
get the right level of insight,

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.930
which becomes very important for
you to resolve that issue once

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:55.760
and forever.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:59.210
With that, let me just
quickly get into a demo.

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:01.630
If we can switch.

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:02.130
Awesome.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:06.390
So this is my beautiful
Gmail background.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:07.560
So you can see--

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:13.660
imagine in this case that I am
the ops person on a schedule,

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:16.890
and I've just recently
received an alert.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:20.480
So, from my perspective, I
want to be able to go in.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:22.990
And I can see that
there's a alert which

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:25.490
was filed for high latency.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:28.380
And this alert tells
me what's going on.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:32.120
You can see the
organization within Apigee

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:33.860
that's encountering the error.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:38.870
It tells me that it
sustained latency

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.780
above what our threshold is.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.510
And so I would like to go
and take a look at that.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:46.100
Once I click on View Details--

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:49.550
and I'm hoping the demo
gods are with us today.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:50.430
Looks like they are.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:51.140
OK.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.610
So this is the very
first entry point.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:54.920
I got a alert.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.090
And I came in.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:58.400
And right away I
can see that there

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:00.760
are some issues with latency.

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:03.370
So this alert kind of
gives me some insight

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:04.640
into what's going on.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:08.660
But more importantly, as
I start to scroll down,

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:13.850
these panels start to give
you some more, deeper insight.

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:17.560
So in this case,
as an example, I

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:21.310
can see that this
particular proxy--

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:22.960
the one which I
got alerted for--

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:25.432
has some high number of errors.

00:10:25.432 --> 00:10:26.890
And I can click on
one of the cells

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:30.680
out here to go and figure
out what's going on.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:33.190
So in this case, it
starts to give me

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:35.740
some insights into
the total latency

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:38.090
and where the error could be.

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:42.560
Now this alert is
extremely powerful for me

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:44.097
to take a peek on
what's going on.

00:10:44.097 --> 00:10:45.680
But I would like to
go and investigate

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.420
this thing a little bit more.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:49.970
So if I click on
the Investigate tab,

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:51.410
this starts to
give me what I mean

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:54.600
by the broad-spectrum
aspects of the platform.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.380
So in this case, I
can see that there

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.960
are different
kinds of dimensions

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:01.130
that I can compare against.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.080
We provide three
different panels here.

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.930
So I can compare Fault Code,
which could be something coming

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.720
right out of an API proxy.

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:09.740
Maybe you've written
up a new policy,

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:10.970
and that threw an error.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.270
I see a very high number of
inverted access token errors

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:15.595
out here.

00:11:15.595 --> 00:11:17.220
So as the operator,
I'm really curious.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.100
I want to be able to
click on this time zone,

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:21.230
and then drill down.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:25.720
What it tells me is that there
is a specific API proxy--

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:28.420
in this case, the one
that I got alerted on--

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:30.260
that is having this error.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:33.850
But also, out here, if we have
developer applications which

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:37.690
are consuming it, you will start
to get a distribution by not

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:40.490
only the developer app,
but also by the target back

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:41.680
in the [? re-hitting. ?]

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:43.930
This is very powerful,
because not only can I

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:45.800
do this kind of analysis.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.360
But more importantly, I
can go out here, and also

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:50.570
examine the logs.

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:53.110
And this is what I meant
by contextual insights

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:54.550
and drill down.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:57.520
Typically, we see
customers doing this thing

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:00.340
from a combination of two
or three different tools.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:02.620
You may get alerted of an issue.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.480
You may then go and
determine that I

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.180
need to go find out and
[INAUDIBLE] the logs.

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:09.280
But in this case, you
have all that information

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.820
at your fingertips.

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:14.560
So in this case, I now start
getting some pretty detailed

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:17.780
information about where
the error is coming from,

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:20.720
which API proxy.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:22.340
And I also, more
importantly, have

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:26.810
a Message ID that can be used to
correlate across other systems.

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:27.410
Right?

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:29.090
So this is just a
very simple example

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:31.850
of how you can use
these capabilities.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:36.050
Additionally, we have also
integrated custom reporting

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:38.180
into this tool.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:40.550
And so we talk to customers.

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:43.280
They examine this
very classical,

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:45.800
real-time to trending continuum.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:47.540
And what that
means is, of course

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:49.670
they want to be
alerted in real time.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:52.130
But they also want to
be able to then navigate

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:54.170
to perhaps a time frame
when the error actually

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:55.290
started occurring.

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.520
And this is a very common
scenario in use cases

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:00.280
where, let's say, you
deployed an API proxy.

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:00.797
OK?

00:13:00.797 --> 00:13:02.630
And let's say your
latency threshold happens

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:05.960
to be 10 seconds, right?

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:10.190
But this API proxy,
deployed, starts

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:12.620
to creep up five
seconds, six seconds,

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:14.630
slowly over a period of time.

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:18.110
You just happen to notice
that the second week that it

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:20.420
crept over 10 seconds,
you got alerted.

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:25.310
But this continuum of
real time to trending now

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:28.610
gives you the ability to be able
to navigate that and see when

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.470
the error actually occurred.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:32.420
You just got alerted of
this thing right now,

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:33.920
but that gives you
deeper insights

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:36.117
into when that happened.

00:13:36.117 --> 00:13:37.700
So I'll just cover
a couple of screens

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:41.400
real quick out here this is
the Dashboard view, where

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.920
you can get some high-level
access into the total traffic,

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:48.530
the API which have the
top number of errors,

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:50.790
top latencies.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.950
And then you can look into
different aspects out here.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:56.520
Collections that are
[INAUDIBLE] of an APIs together.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:14:00.000
What we have found is we
have several of our customers

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.070
who have operational teams
who owned those APIs.

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:04.650
They may be a central
operations team

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:07.290
who want to be the
dispatcher, if you would.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:09.720
But then they monitor
groups of APIs

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:10.980
of certain characteristics.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.660
You can do that easily
with Collections.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:18.130
Another view I would like to
talk about is the Recent view.

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:22.360
In this case, this is
a very interesting view

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:26.140
where you can do what we
call is NOC-like analysis,

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:27.740
like a Network Operation Center.

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:30.210
So in this case,
this heat map view

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:33.160
gives you a peek into what's
going on, what's wrong.

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.310
And if I click on this
environment as an example,

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:37.450
double click on
this, it now starts

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:40.240
to give me a drill down
of different API proxies.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:43.450
So I can come from the top
and see something as an error,

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:47.070
but then very quickly start
to drill on certain scenarios,

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:50.830
on certain API proxies, and then
being able to kind of examine

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:53.380
all the contextual information.

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:55.320
So think of this
as, if something

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.110
is wrong at a high level,
I can drill, drill, drill,

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:01.610
and go to the parts and
figure out what's broken.

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:03.620
The last part I want
to show out here

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:05.410
is the alerting capability.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:08.420
The fact that I got an alert
from something out here

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.360
is something that you
can easily configure.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:13.820
So here is an example of the
alert that we have configured,

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:14.983
that fired.

00:15:14.983 --> 00:15:17.150
In this case, again, we
have some pretty interesting

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:18.080
heuristics.

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:21.140
You can go and do alerting
on different metrics--

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:22.470
in this case, latency.

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.920
But you can pick
status codes, for code

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:28.230
which may be emanating
from certain proxies.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:31.380
And, more importantly, you can
see this pretty interesting

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:32.830
chart data out here.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.140
It's called the Condition data.

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:38.340
So this gives you, as
the operator, the ability

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:40.470
to set the alert
at the right level

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:42.310
so that it's not too noisy.

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:44.520
So we aggregate
information, give you

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:49.080
what the latency is, and you
can see the chart and determine,

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:52.210
well, I really want I get
notified at this point in time.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:52.810
OK?

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:55.030
So with this, I'm going to--

00:15:55.030 --> 00:16:00.740
